PROGRESSIVE VERTICAL SLICES & MINIMAL DEPENDENCY MANDATE:
1. User stories MUST be structured as progressive vertical slices of the application, NOT horizontal architectural layers. The goal is to exploit user-story level parallelism.
2. Foundational Scaffolding in US-001 (Observable Primary Entrypoint First): The very first user story (US-001) MUST establish the minimal, runnable vertical slice and foundational project scaffolding (directory layout, build configuration, REAL primary executable/daemon/library entry point with baseline argument parsing or health check dispatch, and shared domain data types/interfaces) needed by all subsequent user stories (depends_on: []). Task 1 of US-001 MUST implement a real, compiling primary entry point rather than leaving main() or the server daemon as an empty placeholder stub.
3. Minimal Inter-Story Dependencies (Maximum Parallelism): User stories MUST have as few dependencies as possible. Subsequent feature user stories SHOULD depend ONLY on US-001 (e.g. depends_on: ["US-001"]) so that independent architectural tracks (e.g. domain algorithms, CLI commands, HTTP/REST endpoints, web UI, storage adapters) can execute concurrently in parallel worker branches without waiting for one another. The goal is to exploit user-story level parallelism.
4. Avoid Unnecessary Linear Chaining: Do NOT chain stories linearly (e.g. US-003 depending on US-002 depending on US-001) unless a story strictly requires concrete logic or database tables that only the immediate predecessor provides.
5. All Subsequent Stories Runnable: Subsequent user stories progressively add vertical feature slices with end-to-end command dispatch and co-located tests, ensuring the codebase is 100% runnable, testable, and demonstrable at every single story.
6. Comprehensive Functional Subsystem Coverage Mandate: You MUST decompose all major functional subsystems declared in SPEC.md (e.g. network/wire protocols, data storage/indexing, command dispatchers, and persistence) into distinct functional user stories. For extensive specifications declaring distinct data structures, command families, or protocols (e.g. key-value, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, streams, transactions, pub/sub), you MUST allocate dedicated feature stories to each domain capability so that 100% of the functional command matrix and wire surface declared in SPEC.md is fully represented across the roadmap before the final hardening story. Never truncate, omit, or compress multi-subsystem specifications into only a skeleton and a generic hardening story. Every command, feature group, or API endpoint declared in SPEC.md must have an explicit user story and Definition of Done with executable behavioral contracts.

