MODULAR FILE DECOMPOSITION & ANTI-MONOLITH MANDATE (SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY ARCHITECTURE):
1. Single Responsibility & File Separation: Decompose source code files and test files by single responsibility, domain capability, and distinct command/entity (e.g. separate files/modules for individual commands, handlers, codecs, parsers, models, storage engines, controllers, endpoints, and utility functions). Under NO circumstances should an application, subsystem, or command suite be consolidated into a lone, gigantic monolithic file (e.g., a single massive commands.py, handlers.go, server.rs, or all-in-one test file).
2. Override of Monolithic Specifications: Even if SPEC.md does NOT mention any file layout, or even if SPEC.md explicitly specifies or implies a lone gigantic source file (such as a single monolithic src/commands.py or all commands in main.py), you MUST OVERRIDE that design. When generating user stories and when refining SPEC.md (via 'refine_spec'), you MUST prescribe and decompose the architecture into a modular layout with fine-grained directories and per-responsibility files (e.g. src/commands/base.py, src/commands/<cmd>.py, and co-located tests/unit/commands/test_<cmd>.py).
3. Maximize Parallelism & Eliminate Merge Conflicts: Modular per-responsibility files ensure that parallel worker branches and worktrees can implement separate commands, models, and features simultaneously with zero git merge conflicts, minimal context/token overhead, fast compilation/execution, and clean atomic git merges.
4. Definition of Done Explicit File Paths: The Definition of Done (DoD) in EVERY user story MUST explicitly enumerate the decomposed, modular target file paths (both implementation files and co-located unit test files) required for that story's scope, preventing downstream planners and generators from falling back to monolithic files.
5. Language & Framework Agnostic: This rule applies across all programming languages, toolchains, and project archetypes without exception (e.g., Go, Python, Rust, TypeScript, C, C++, Java).

DEFINITION OF DONE (DoD) & CONTRACT MANDATE:
Every user story content generated or refined MUST include an explicit, language-agnostic "Definition of Done (DoD)" section containing:
1. Public Interface & Entry Point Contracts: Specify exact public API method/module signatures AND binary executable paths (if a CLI application or utility).
2. Standard I/O & Output Formatting Invariants: Specify exact stdout/stderr output strings, error message prefixes (e.g. "Error: ..."), exit status codes (0 for success, non-zero for error), and interactive REPL prompt characters.
3. Explicit Data Representation & Number Formats: Specify number precision (integer vs float output representations), edge case boundaries, and empty input behaviors.
4. Comprehensive Scenario & Edge Case Matrix: Include input validation edge cases, boundary values, error conditions, and unexpected input handling scenarios.
5. Verification Criteria: Mandate 100% pass rate on unit, integration, and black-box E2E test suites with zero test failures. Do NOT include zero-linter-error requirements in intermediate feature stories; linting is non-blocking advisory during feature delivery.
6. Chicago-School Testing & In-Memory Test Double Invariant: DoD MUST mandate Chicago-school testing (testing observable state transitions on real domain objects) with Dependency Injection for external boundaries (databases, network sockets, background queues, key-value stores, external APIs). Domain and component tasks MUST author and use In-Memory Test Doubles (e.g. InMemoryRepository, FakeQueue, InMemoryStore, SQLite ':memory:', in-memory mock clients) for unit/integration tests. Direct binding to live network sockets, real external databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL), or live background broker processes is strictly reserved for the final hardening/E2E acceptance story.
7. Black-Box E2E All-Scenario & Anti-Tautology Invariant: DoD MUST mandate black-box E2E tests covering all scenarios (happy paths, boundary conditions, error exit codes, and output formatting) executed with socket readiness polling rather than static sleep delays. All HTTP requests, socket connections, TCP client calls, and network reads in test cases MUST enforce a maximum timeout of 2 seconds (assuming tests are broken if they don't respond within 2 seconds). E2E tests MUST physically execute client commands against the running application and assert domain payloads; tautological tests (such as scripts that only connect/disconnect, test process signals without testing protocol commands, or generic import tests) are strictly forbidden.
   - Specific E2E Scenario Per Feature Mandate: The DoD MUST mandate that for EACH feature, command, flag, and API capability described in the story (e.g. for key-value stores: separate test scenarios for PING, ECHO, GET, SET, DEL, EXISTS, EXPIRE, TTL, KEYS, INCR, DECR, FLUSHALL; for chess: separate scenarios for uci, isready, position, go, perft), there MUST be at least one explicit, runnable end-to-end black-box test scenario dedicated to exercising that exact command/feature with realistic payloads and asserting the expected response format. A generic smoke test or walking skeleton test is strictly insufficient; every command must have its own verified test scenario.
8. Deterministic Time & Mock Clock Invariants: Every feature involving time, timers, dates, TTL, expiration, or wall-clock schedules MUST mandate deterministic mock clocks (e.g. Store(clock=FakeClock())) in the DoD to eliminate 1-second boundary assertion race conditions.
9. Pinned Modular Directory & Required File Paths (Anti-Monolith Mandate): The Definition of Done MUST explicitly list the exact repository-relative file paths for every module, command, handler, model, and co-located test required by the story. If SPEC.md specifies a pinned directory layout, enforce it, BUT if SPEC.md specifies or implies a lone gigantic source file or lacks a modular directory layout, you MUST decompose it into fine-grained per-responsibility files (e.g. src/commands/<cmd>.<ext>, tests/unit/commands/test_<cmd>.<ext>). Every single required modular file path must be explicitly named in the DoD so downstream planners and generators implement atomic, single-responsibility files without regressions. Modular dependencies and imports MUST form a strict directed acyclic graph (DAG): internal handlers, submodules, and leaf domain primitives must NEVER import from parent package facades, index modules, or coordinator entrypoints to prevent circular dependency deadlocks.
